'''''Asplenium septentrionale''''' is a species of [[fern]] known by the common names '''northern spleenwort''' and '''forked spleenwort'''. It is native to Europe, Asia and western North America, where it grows on rocks. Its long, slender leaves give it a distinctive appearance. Three subspecies exist, corresponding to a [[tetraploid]] and a [[diploid]] [[wiktionary:cytotype|cytotype]] and their [[triploid]] [[hybrid (biology)|hybrid]].

==Description==
''Asplenium septentrionale'' is a small fern which grows in dense clusters superficially resembling tufts of grass. The long, dark stems support narrow, leathery leaf blades, which may appear slightly forked at the tip. The fronds are monomorphic, with no difference in shape or size between fertile and sterile fronds.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}

The rhizome from which the many leaves of each plant spring is about 1&nbsp;millimetre in diameter, and covered with scales. The scales are narrowly triangular, and range in color from black to a dark reddish brown. They are {{convert|2|to|4|mm|in|sigfig=1}} long, 0.3 to 0.6&nbsp;millimetres wide, and entire (untoothed) at the edges. The [[stipe (botany)|stipe]]s (the stalks of the leaf, below the blade) are reddish brown at the base, fading to green above. They range from {{convert|2|to|13|cm|in|sigfig=1}} in length, and are about 2 to 5 times the length of the leaf blade itself.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}

The leaf blades are narrow, with parallel edges following the stem, ranging from {{convert|0.5|to|4|cm|in|sigfig=1}} in length and {{convert|0.1|to|0.4|cm|in|sigfig=1}} in width. They come to a point at both base and tip, and have a leathery texture. They are often divided into pinnae (leaflets) near the tip, usually two (but sometimes four). These pinnae are sharply angled towards the tip of the blade, giving it a forked appearance. They also come to a point at the tip, and have a few widely spaced, irregular teeth. The [[rachis]] (central axis of the leaf) is green and shiny, and the leaves, including the rachis, are free of hairs or scales. Fertile and sterile fronds are the same in appearance; in fertile fronds, the [[sorus|sori]] are linear, parallel to the edges of the pinna, usually two or more per pinna.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}} The sori are covered by thin, pale tan [[indusium|indusia]], with entire edges.{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}} It contains 64 spores per [[sporangium]], and most [[sporophyte]]s have a chromosome number of 2''n''=144 (a [[tetraploid]]).{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}

[[File:Asplenium septentrionale 070906a.jpg|left|200px|thumb|Fertile leaves of ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' showing brown sori on underside]]

Individual plants have an abundant number of leaves, forming dense tufts from a rhizome of about 1&nbsp;millimetre in diameter, and sometimes mats on flat rocks.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}}

''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' is easily distinguished from other related ferns by its narrow blades, often forked at the tip. The presence of sori on fertile leaves distinguishes it from the vegetative material of a grass or sedge.

==Taxonomy==
Originally named ''Acrostichum septentrionale'' by [[Carl Linnaeus|Linnaeus]] in 1753, the species was placed in genus ''[[Asplenium]]'' by [[Georg Franz Hoffmann]] in 1796.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}} The species has twice been placed in genera segregated from ''[[Asplenium]]'': as ''Chamaefilix septentrionalis'' by [[Oliver Atkins Farwell|Farwell]] in 1931,{{sfn|Farwell|1931|p=268}} and as ''Tarachia septentrionalis'' by Momose in 1960.{{sfn|Momose|1960}} Neither combination was widely accepted and current authorities do not recognize these segregate genera.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}} A global phylogeny of ''Asplenium'' published in 2020 divided the genus into eleven clades,{{sfn|Xu|Zhang|Rothfels|Smith|2020|p=27}} which were given informal names pending further taxonomic study. ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' belongs to the "''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' subclade" of the "''Schaffneria'' clade".{{sfn|Xu|Zhang|Rothfels|Smith|2020|p=36}} The ''Schaffneria'' clade has a worldwide distribution, and members vary widely in form and habitat.{{sfn|Xu|Zhang|Rothfels|Smith|2020|p=42}} Most members of the ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' subclade have small leaves with a long green stalk, entire or divided into two or three segments, and always grow on rocks.{{sfn|Xu|Zhang|Rothfels|Smith|2020|p=43}} Other members of the subclade include the European ''[[Asplenium seelosii|A.&nbsp;seelosii]]'' and ''[[Asplenium celtibericum|A.&nbsp;celtibericum]]'' and an undescribed species from Venezuela.{{sfn|Xu|Zhang|Rothfels|Smith|2020|p=36}}

In 1980, the discovery of [[diploid]] specimens prompted the division of the species into ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' subsp. ''septentrionale'', the tetraploid population, and ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' subsp. ''caucasicum'', the diploid population. The diploids are limited to southwest Asia, while the tetraploid population has a [[circumboreal]] distribution. The tetraploid subspecies is believed to have arisen from the diploid subspecies by [[autopolyploid]]y. The two subspecies are very similar in form, but can be distinguished by the narrower blades of ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale''&nbsp;subsp.&nbsp;''caucasicum'' (to {{convert|0.12|cm|in|2}} wide) and its smaller spores (averaging 34&nbsp;μm in diameter). In 2003, Ronald Viane and [[Tadeus Reichstein]] elevated this subspecies to a species, as ''A.&nbsp;caucasicum'', and described a sterile [[triploid]] formed by hybridization of the tetraploid and diploid, identifiable by its abortive spores. They designated the hybrid ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;dirense''.{{sfn|Viane|Reichstein|2003|pp=89–90}} However, in his taxonomic revision of Indian pteridophytes, Christopher Fraser-Jenkins reduced all of these taxa to subspecific rank, with the hybrid ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;dirense'' becoming the [[nothospecies|nothosubspecies]] ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' subsp.&nbsp;''rehmanii''.

''Asplenium septentrionale'' can hybridize with maidenhair spleenwort (''A.&nbsp;trichomanes'') to form the hybrid ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;alternifolium'', with [[Asplenium foreziense|''A.&nbsp;foreziense'']] to form ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;costei'', with black spleenwort ([[Asplenium adiantum-nigrum|''A.&nbsp;adiantum-nigrum'']]) to form ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;contrei'', with [[Asplenium obovatum subsp. lanceolatum|''A.&nbsp;obovatum'' subsp. ''lanceolatum'']] to form ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;souchei'', and with wall-rue ([[Asplenium ruta-muraria|''A.&nbsp;ruta-muraria'']]) to form ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;murbeckii'' and ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;tavelio''. ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;murbeckii'' can back-cross with ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' to form ''A.&nbsp;×&nbsp;hungaricum''.

==Distribution and habitat==
''Asplenium septentrionale'' subsp. ''septentrionale'' is found in Europe and Asia from the [[Macaronesian Islands]] east through Europe, [[western Asia]], the former [[USSR]], northern India, parts of western and central China ([[Xinjiang]], [[Tibet]], and [[Shaanxi]]), and [[Taiwan]].{{sfn|Viane|Reichstein|2003|pp=89–90}} It is also found in [[North America]], principally in the [[Rocky Mountains]] of the western United States, but also in the [[Black Hills]] of [[South Dakota]]–[[Wyoming]]. It also occurs in the mountains of the [[Cascade Range]] and the [[Sierra Nevada (U.S.)|Sierra Nevada]], from [[Oregon]] south through [[California]] into [[Baja California]]. Populations occur to the eastward in western [[Texas]] and near the tip of the [[Oklahoma Panhandle]], and two very [[disjunct distribution|disjunct]] stations have been located on [[shale]] in [[Monroe County, West Virginia|Monroe County]] and [[Hardy County, West Virginia]].{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}}

''Asplenium septentrionale'' subsp. ''caucasicum'' has been reported from [[Georgia (country)|Georgia]], [[Iran]], [[Pakistan]] and [[Turkey]]. The type specimen of ''A.&nbsp;septentrionale'' subsp. ''rehmanii'' came from Pakistan, near the [[Lowari Pass]].{{sfn|Viane|Reichstein|2003|pp=89–90}}

''Asplenium septentrionale'' is [[epipetric]] and can be found in crevices of rocks, around boulders, and on cliffs.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}} It can be found on a variety of substrates,{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}} including [[granitic]] rocks{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}} and [[limestone]].{{sfn|Hoshizaki|Moran|2001|p=204}} The West Virginia stations are on [[shale]].{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}} In the United States, it can be found growing at altitudes from {{convert|700|to|2900|m|ft}}.{{sfn|Wagner|Moran|Werth|1993}}

==Cultivation==
''Asplenium septentrionale'' is hardy to [[USDA Zone]] 4. It is best grown in partial sun or medium light in moist to dry potting mix.{{sfn|Hoshizaki|Moran|2001|p=204}}{{sfn|Lellinger|1985|p=233}} It requires good drainage and little watering. Sensitive to root disturbance, it is difficult to transplant and establish, and is rarely sold by nurseries.{{sfn|Hoshizaki|Moran|2001|p=204}}

